Ladybird ends a line that breaks after a soft hyphen in U+2010 where the
primary font has a glyph for it, and in U+002D otherwise. Blink makes
that choice in ComputedStyle::HyphenString(), WebKit in ComputedStyle::
hyphenString(), and Gecko in gfxFontGroup::MakeHyphenTextRun(). Run in
Chrome 157, Firefox 151, and WebKit 26.5, a page of soft hyphens
(min-content and zero widths, “hyphens: none” on a block and on a span
around the soft hyphen, RTL text, letter-spacing, a soft hyphen that
ends an inline item, and a break that fits only once the hyphen moves to
the next line) gives the same lines and widths in all three, and in
Ladybird, but for two cases. Firefox, WebKit, and Ladybird letter-space
the hyphen, and Chrome doesn’t. And Chrome, WebKit, and Ladybird break
after a soft hyphen that ends an inline item, and Firefox doesn’t.

An out-of-flow box between a soft hyphen and the text after it leaves
that break in place, and Ladybird still leaves room for the hyphen on
the line that ends there. Chrome 157 and Firefox 151 fit such a line in
its box, under “overflow-wrap: anywhere” and after a space alike. WebKit
26.5 does that after a space, but lets the line overflow its box under
“overflow-wrap: anywhere”.
